00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:09.820
- Yeah, no, that was the scariest thing.

00:09:09.820 --> 00:09:13.500
Because I wasn't comfortable
with that unknown space

00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:14.333
like how you are.

00:09:14.333 --> 00:09:16.626
You've always been in this lane.

00:09:16.626 --> 00:09:17.459
- [Gary] Yeah, that's my world.

00:09:17.459 --> 00:09:18.340
- For me, that was never my world.

00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:21.070
I like that safe, I was tricking myself.

00:09:21.070 --> 00:09:23.630
I thought I was really safe
being in corporate America

00:09:23.630 --> 00:09:24.690
which is bullshit.

00:09:24.690 --> 00:09:26.440
Because any company can be sold tomorrow.

00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:28.240
And you only have your brand
- [Gary] Correct.

00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:30.350
- and you, and your
experiences, and your network.

00:09:30.350 --> 00:09:33.600
So you are not safe unless
you are actually doing it.

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:36.410
I feel so much safer now
that I work for myself.

00:09:36.410 --> 00:09:37.243
- [Gary] 'Cause you're in control.

00:09:37.243 --> 00:09:38.636
- I'm in control.

00:09:38.636 --> 00:09:39.469
I trust my boss.

00:09:39.469 --> 00:09:40.670
You know, I'm not gonna sell the company.

00:09:40.670 --> 00:09:41.647
I'm good.

00:09:41.647 --> 00:09:46.170
It's a seismic shift to
think about how I believe--

00:09:46.170 --> 00:09:47.480
- What's the first thing you thought?

00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:49.641
How did you get fired, in person?

00:09:49.641 --> 00:09:52.010
- Yeah, in a two minute meeting.

00:09:52.010 --> 00:09:53.542
In two minutes.
- [Gary] With HR?

00:09:53.542 --> 00:09:55.610
- No, with the new CEO.

00:09:55.610 --> 00:09:58.190
The CEO I had worked for
for 14 years became ill.

00:09:58.190 --> 00:10:00.480
He elevated his daughter to replace him.

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:01.757
And she immediately said,

00:10:01.757 --> 00:10:03.760
"Come over, I need to
have a meeting with you."

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:06.420
And she basically passed
two memos in front of me

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:09.237
and said, "I don't need a
chief revenue officer anymore.

00:10:09.237 --> 00:10:11.580
"I don't need you, which
memo would you like to sign?"

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:13.640
And one said Heather Monohan's been fired

00:10:13.640 --> 00:10:17.050
and the other one said Heather
Monohan, wonderful employee,

00:10:17.050 --> 00:10:17.883
blah, blah, blah.

00:10:17.883 --> 00:10:19.670
But I knew, this wasn't my first rodeo.

00:10:19.670 --> 00:10:20.503
I knew that there was--

00:10:20.503 --> 00:10:23.180
- Did you have any
relationship with her, prior?

00:10:23.180 --> 00:10:26.814
- Well she was the CFO.
- [Gary] Got it, got it.

00:10:26.814 --> 00:10:27.877
- And I was the CRO.

00:10:27.877 --> 00:10:29.244
So we were like, she
wanted to cut everything

00:10:29.244 --> 00:10:30.180
and I wanted to spend everything.

00:10:30.180 --> 00:10:31.066
- I got it, of course, that makes sense.

00:10:31.066 --> 00:10:31.940
- [Heather] But I never--

00:10:31.940 --> 00:10:33.164
- Did you know when she called you over

00:10:33.164 --> 00:10:33.997
that it was kaputskies or did you hope--

00:10:33.997 --> 00:10:35.710
- No, 'cause she's a smart woman.

00:10:35.710 --> 00:10:38.407
She's very analytical, she's a CFO.

00:10:38.407 --> 00:10:39.950
She's the antithesis of me.

00:10:39.950 --> 00:10:41.327
But I thought she knew,

00:10:41.327 --> 00:10:42.733
Heather's good at her job.
- [Gary] The yin and the yang.

00:10:42.733 --> 00:10:44.350
- I'm not gonna fire her.

00:10:44.350 --> 00:10:47.488
I figured she was gonna rough
me up a little to intimidate.

00:10:47.488 --> 00:10:48.580
- Or try to get aligned.

00:10:48.580 --> 00:10:50.064
- [Heather] Well I didn't
think that was gonna happen.

00:10:50.064 --> 00:10:50.900
- So, were you shocked?

00:10:50.900 --> 00:10:54.563
- I was, I cried the whole
three hour ride home.

00:10:56.900 --> 00:10:59.690
- Do you have it in you in
that scenario, what's funny

00:10:59.690 --> 00:11:03.360
about me, is every time anything happens,

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:06.120
I start the process of
worst case scenario.

00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:09.370
Dustin emails me and says, "I
need three minutes with you."

00:11:09.370 --> 00:11:12.220
The first, this is real, it just happened.

00:11:12.220 --> 00:11:15.110
The first thing I think
is, I hope he's not sick.

00:11:15.110 --> 00:11:16.600
I go so funny.
- [Heather] Oh my gosh.

00:11:16.600 --> 00:11:18.720
- I go into what's the
worst that could happen?

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:20.080
I hope he's not sick.

00:11:20.080 --> 00:11:23.040
I hope something weird didn't happen,

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:25.300
like John did something weird to him

00:11:25.300 --> 00:11:27.504
and it needs to go to
HR, that's the next--

00:11:27.504 --> 00:11:28.337
(Heather laughing)

00:11:28.337 --> 00:11:29.700
To me, it's in this order, something,

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:31.840
that person, or their family is sick.

00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:35.190
Next, something super
politically incorrect, legal,

00:11:35.190 --> 00:11:37.930
sexual harassment, that's
the next worst thing.

00:11:37.930 --> 00:11:40.480
Third is, a good employee wants to leave.

00:11:40.480 --> 00:11:42.600
So if like a good employee
says, "I need to see you,"

00:11:42.600 --> 00:11:44.210
that's the third thing I think.

00:11:44.210 --> 00:11:45.420
But that's all I think.

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:46.253
I don't think like,

00:11:46.253 --> 00:11:47.935
"Oh, it's gonna be a
five minute check in."

00:11:47.935 --> 00:11:49.740
This is, by the way,
everyone who's listening,

00:11:49.740 --> 00:11:52.210
this is the shit of owning your shit.

00:11:52.210 --> 00:11:53.780
This is actually the way you think.

00:11:53.780 --> 00:11:55.090
This is why it's interesting what you,

00:11:55.090 --> 00:11:58.180
like it's crazy to me, but
it makes so much sense.

00:11:58.180 --> 00:11:59.500
Which is why I'm jumping on this point,

00:11:59.500 --> 00:12:01.390
I never really thought about this.

00:12:01.390 --> 00:12:03.710
The only thing I would've
thought when she wanted

00:12:03.710 --> 00:12:06.090
to see me is, "I'm getting fired."

00:12:06.090 --> 00:12:08.220
But what I just realized
based on your reaction,

00:12:08.220 --> 00:12:10.140
literally it just hit me, is like, right,

00:12:10.140 --> 00:12:13.010
when you're completely
accountable to yourself,

00:12:13.010 --> 00:12:14.810
which is what you're figuring you now,

00:12:14.810 --> 00:12:17.878
all the greatness of loving
your boss and feeling safe?

00:12:17.878 --> 00:12:18.711
- [Heather] Yeah.

00:12:18.711 --> 00:12:20.690
- The shit part is, the way
my brain actually works.

00:12:20.690 --> 00:12:21.890
Listen to what I'm telling you.

00:12:21.890 --> 00:12:24.610
Every time anyone reaches
out to me, in order,

00:12:24.610 --> 00:12:28.750
I think about, terminal illness
for them or their loved one,

00:12:28.750 --> 00:12:32.530
sexual harassment or stealing
or something horrible,

00:12:32.530 --> 00:12:35.260
third is they're leaving
and we value them.

00:12:35.260 --> 00:12:37.340
That is literally the
order that my head goes.

00:12:37.340 --> 00:12:40.090
Then it's like, "Hey, Gary,
can we get five hundred bucks

00:12:40.090 --> 00:12:43.000
for a T.G.I. Friday's party
next Thursday for the team?"

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:44.311
I'm like, "Yes!"

00:12:44.311 --> 00:12:45.216
(Heather laughing)

00:12:45.216 --> 00:12:48.310
That's why I'm always in a
good mood when it's anything

00:12:48.310 --> 00:12:50.810
but three, and the third
one's not even that scary,

00:12:50.810 --> 00:12:53.750
'cause genuinely, on the
record, for anybody from Vayner

00:12:53.750 --> 00:12:55.680
that hears this, if one
feels like they're ready

00:12:55.680 --> 00:12:57.320
to move on, I wanna help them.

00:12:57.320 --> 00:12:59.310
It's the first two that
scare the fuck out of me

00:12:59.310 --> 00:13:01.130
and always what I think.

00:13:01.130 --> 00:13:03.360
Every time a client reaches
out, we're getting fired.

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:05.550
Never we're getting
more growth of business.

00:13:05.550 --> 00:13:07.370
Macro, I'm optimistic.

00:13:07.370 --> 00:13:09.780
Macro, I'm like, we're
gonna win, we're the best.

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:11.500
Micro...

00:13:11.500 --> 00:13:12.950
Micro, I'm not.

00:13:12.950 --> 00:13:14.050
- That means something big.

00:13:14.050 --> 00:13:15.797
I don't know what it is,
I'm not a psychologist,

00:13:15.797 --> 00:13:17.450
but it's gotta be something.

00:13:17.450 --> 00:13:18.283
- Let me tell you what
it means in your world.

00:13:18.283 --> 00:13:20.120
When you're walking in there, micro,

00:13:20.120 --> 00:13:21.620
she's abut to fucking fire me.

00:13:21.620 --> 00:13:24.010
Macro, I'm fucking a capable human being,

00:13:24.010 --> 00:13:25.270
I'm gonna fucking figure it out.

00:13:25.270 --> 00:13:27.090
Whether I'm gonna get another fucking job

00:13:27.090 --> 00:13:28.270
that's gonna pay me more,

00:13:28.270 --> 00:13:30.552
or I'm gonna start my own career
and fucking speak at TedX.

00:13:30.552 --> 00:13:32.640
Got it?

00:13:32.640 --> 00:13:34.670
- Yeah, but I was the opposite of that.

00:13:34.670 --> 00:13:35.580
- I understand.
- [Heather] That's crazy.

00:13:35.580 --> 00:13:37.821
- Which is what I'm thinking,

00:13:37.821 --> 00:13:39.100
why I'm saying this so aggressively is

00:13:39.100 --> 00:13:41.110
'cause there's two people
listening right now,

00:13:41.110 --> 00:13:42.770
in this show, in the
framework of business,

00:13:42.770 --> 00:13:44.810
entrepreneurs and employees.

00:13:44.810 --> 00:13:47.660
And it's like an interesting
debate for people that are,

00:13:47.660 --> 00:13:49.867
like, for example, there's a lot of people

00:13:49.867 --> 00:13:53.010
that are employees right
now that are thinking

00:13:53.010 --> 00:13:56.110
about making the jump where
they listen to what I just said,

00:13:56.110 --> 00:13:58.527
and are saying, "Do I wanna
live a life where I have

00:13:58.527 --> 00:14:02.510
"to always be that on and
anxious at all times?"

00:14:02.510 --> 00:14:04.760
'Cause yes, I am a firefighter.

00:14:04.760 --> 00:14:06.700
Like, what's amazing
about your life right now

00:14:06.700 --> 00:14:07.710
is you're in control.

00:14:07.710 --> 00:14:09.320
What's terrible is you're in control.

00:14:09.320 --> 00:14:10.153
- [Heather] Right.

00:14:10.153 --> 00:14:12.180
- Like, if you decide
to chill this morning,

00:14:12.180 --> 00:14:15.190
like if Dustin, I'm just looking at him.

00:14:15.190 --> 00:14:17.550
If Dustin's just not feeling it today,

00:14:17.550 --> 00:14:20.270
and I'm pumped all the time
and have days when you wake up,

00:14:20.270 --> 00:14:22.330
and you're just not fully feeling it,

00:14:22.330 --> 00:14:24.510
one could argue that
you can call out sick.

00:14:24.510 --> 00:14:26.040
You can lollygag your day.

00:14:26.040 --> 00:14:27.059
- [Heather] You can.

00:14:27.059 --> 00:14:30.367
- For me, that's, I'm like, "Oh,

00:14:30.367 --> 00:14:32.917
"the reason I haven't had
a sick day in nine years is

00:14:32.917 --> 00:14:34.890
"that's more scary than the lollygagging."

00:14:34.890 --> 00:14:37.580
Obviously if I run a 105 fever,
I'm willing to stay home.

00:14:37.580 --> 00:14:39.160
But all those days, I mean,

00:14:39.160 --> 00:14:42.730
I called out of school
13 to 23 times a year.

00:14:42.730 --> 00:14:45.390
I made pretend I was sick 13 times a year.

00:14:45.390 --> 00:14:47.420
Once I figured out in high
school that the nurse had

00:14:47.420 --> 00:14:50.070
to send you home if you
weren't willing to go back

00:14:50.070 --> 00:14:52.460
to class, legally, it was over.

00:14:52.460 --> 00:14:54.298
I just laid there until they sent me home.

00:14:54.298 --> 00:14:55.131
(Heather chuckling)

00:14:55.131 --> 00:14:56.480
I'm like, "I'm nauseous, I'm
unconscious, I feel weird,

00:14:56.480 --> 00:14:58.320
I'm anxious, get me out of here."

00:14:58.320 --> 00:14:59.960
And then the best part was I'd go outside,

00:14:59.960 --> 00:15:01.740
my mom would have to pick me
up freshman and sophomore year,

00:15:01.740 --> 00:15:03.230
and junior year before I got my car,

00:15:03.230 --> 00:15:05.150
and literally I would never look at her.

00:15:05.150 --> 00:15:06.630
Because the second I
would look into her eyes,

00:15:06.630 --> 00:15:08.551
I would start laughing,
because we both knew the gig.

00:15:08.551 --> 00:15:09.790
- The gig was up.

00:15:09.790 --> 00:15:11.800
- [Gary] Anyway not tattle.

00:15:11.800 --> 00:15:14.360
What happens, you cry all
the way home and then what?

00:15:14.360 --> 00:15:16.960
- I went under a weighted
blanket, grabbed a bottle

00:15:16.960 --> 00:15:17.793
of chardonnay.

00:15:17.793 --> 00:15:19.065
And then I did something--

00:15:19.065 --> 00:15:19.960
- [Gary] How was the chardonnay?

00:15:19.960 --> 00:15:20.830
- It wasn't Empathy.

00:15:20.830 --> 00:15:21.917
It needed to be Empathy.
- [Gary] I know that.

00:15:21.917 --> 00:15:23.077
- Now it's Empathy!

00:15:23.077 --> 00:15:23.910
- [Gary] No, but how was it?

00:15:23.910 --> 00:15:24.743
- Oh, it was good.

00:15:24.743 --> 00:15:25.576
- [Gary] Good, okay.

00:15:25.576 --> 00:15:26.470
- All right, so here's the thing though.

00:15:26.470 --> 00:15:28.570
I did something super smart,
and I had never done it.

00:15:28.570 --> 00:15:31.120
I posted on social media
that I just got fired.

00:15:31.120 --> 00:15:32.477
I just was vulnerable and I just said,

00:15:32.477 --> 00:15:33.980
"I need your help."
- [Gary] You needed something.

00:15:33.980 --> 00:15:34.813
- I needed help.

00:15:34.813 --> 00:15:35.780
I didn't know what to do.

00:15:35.780 --> 00:15:38.040
And it was crazy, Froggy
from the Elvis Duran show,

00:15:38.040 --> 00:15:39.997
tweeted at me, "I wanna help you,

00:15:39.997 --> 00:15:41.560
"is there anyway I can help you?"

00:15:41.560 --> 00:15:44.260
And I tweeted back,
"Yeah, book me on Elvis."

00:15:44.260 --> 00:15:46.060
He booked me right away
to go on the Elvis Show.

00:15:46.060 --> 00:15:47.840
I went on the show and
halfway through the interview,

00:15:47.840 --> 00:15:50.330
Elvis says, "Well obviously
you're writing a book."

00:15:50.330 --> 00:15:51.880
And I said, "Well obviously!"

00:15:51.880 --> 00:15:52.940
But I wasn't.

00:15:52.940 --> 00:15:54.967
So I googled on the plan, on the way home,

00:15:54.967 --> 00:15:55.800
- [Gary] How to write a--
- "How do you write a book?"

00:15:55.800 --> 00:15:56.910
And it says, just write.

00:15:56.910 --> 00:15:58.060
So I was like, "I can do that."

00:15:58.060 --> 00:16:00.040
So I just went home and I
started writing and writing.

00:16:00.040 --> 00:16:02.090
And then, you know, it
took like two months

00:16:02.090 --> 00:16:03.290
and I was done with my book.

00:16:03.290 --> 00:16:05.140
- Did you debate self-publishing?

00:16:05.140 --> 00:16:06.570
'Cause I heard you earlier say that,

00:16:06.570 --> 00:16:08.100
versus getting a book deal?

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:10.430
Was the book deal too long and drawn out

00:16:10.430 --> 00:16:11.370
and you wanted to get it out?

00:16:11.370 --> 00:16:12.850
- Speed to market.
- [Gary] Yeah.

00:16:12.850 --> 00:16:15.510
- Obviously, with my
history in business, I knew,

00:16:15.510 --> 00:16:17.220
speed to market is critical.

00:16:17.220 --> 00:16:19.760
And I've gotta move fast and
I'm gonna move so much faster

00:16:19.760 --> 00:16:21.440
than some traditional publishing house.

00:16:21.440 --> 00:16:22.287
I'm glad that I self-published.

00:16:22.287 --> 00:16:24.512
- [Gary] Did you consider getting a job?

00:16:24.512 --> 00:16:26.180
- Like, going back?

00:16:26.180 --> 00:16:27.345
- [Gary] To a job, yes.

00:16:27.345 --> 00:16:28.350
Not to the company.
- So I had to sign,

00:16:28.350 --> 00:16:30.341
I had to sign a noncompete

00:16:30.341 --> 00:16:31.810
when I took the chief
revenue officer position.

00:16:31.810 --> 00:16:33.840
So for one year, I
couldn't go back to media.

00:16:33.840 --> 00:16:35.240
So I thought to myself,
If I'm gonna start over

00:16:35.240 --> 00:16:37.720
as a rookie somewhere, why
wouldn't I roll the dice

00:16:37.720 --> 00:16:41.190
on me versus, if I'm
gonna go do something new,

00:16:41.190 --> 00:16:42.023
I'll just work for me.
- [Gary] So what happened next

00:16:42.023 --> 00:16:42.970
you write the book.

00:16:42.970 --> 00:16:45.197
- So I write the book and then I google,

00:16:45.197 --> 00:16:46.490
"How do you promote a book?"

00:16:46.490 --> 00:16:48.070
And you speak to promote a book.

00:16:48.070 --> 00:16:49.547
So I was like, "Oh, that's easy.

00:16:49.547 --> 00:16:50.570
"I already know how to speak."

00:16:50.570 --> 00:16:52.000
I didn't know there was
a speaking business.

00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:53.160
I had no idea.

00:16:53.160 --> 00:16:54.310
- [Gary] Me neither before I got into it.

00:16:54.310 --> 00:16:55.720
- It's so crazy to me.

00:16:55.720 --> 00:16:58.210
I always think how many other
things do we not know about

00:16:58.210 --> 00:16:59.524
that we could be great at?

00:16:59.524 --> 00:17:00.820
- [Gary] An uncomfortable amount.

00:17:00.820 --> 00:17:01.817
- So that's what's so exciting about--

00:17:01.817 --> 00:17:04.090
- I mean that's my entire thesis.

00:17:04.090 --> 00:17:07.210
There was some gal in line
in this speech I gave.

00:17:07.210 --> 00:17:09.637
And she's like, "Gary, I
know thesis of taste things

00:17:09.637 --> 00:17:12.583
"in your 20s and figure out what you love

00:17:12.583 --> 00:17:14.487
"or what you're great at
or what to your point."

00:17:14.487 --> 00:17:16.819
And she's like, "But I've done all that.

00:17:16.819 --> 00:17:18.540
"What do I do?"

00:17:18.540 --> 00:17:20.886
And I looked at her, she
was from Belarus too.

00:17:20.886 --> 00:17:23.320
So I was like, you know, kind
of like, from the homeland.

00:17:23.320 --> 00:17:25.340
And I'm like, "Let me get this straight,

00:17:25.340 --> 00:17:27.780
you've decided that you've
done everything on Earth,

00:17:27.780 --> 00:17:28.613
and you now know every."

00:17:28.613 --> 00:17:30.837
Like, it was just funny
to watch her realize,

00:17:30.837 --> 00:17:32.517
"Oh I've only done nine things.

00:17:32.517 --> 00:17:34.317
"And there's nine billion things."

00:17:35.690 --> 00:17:37.130
There's so many things!

00:17:37.130 --> 00:17:39.510
- Well it's like, I was just
mentioning to Marcia Kilgore

00:17:39.510 --> 00:17:41.360
how she's disrupted the beauty business.

00:17:41.360 --> 00:17:42.193
Look at that.

00:17:42.193 --> 00:17:43.860
This is someone who has
been in the beauty business

00:17:43.860 --> 00:17:45.439
for a couple of decades
